Z||000000||gtsonly||05-22-2001||07:25 PM||||I was reading somewhere that the fiero at 80 mph or something like that created 100 pounds of lift. Is this true and if so is there a way or has anyone modified the front to create downforce or am I just all messed up?<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||05-22-2001||08:27 PM||||I have heard that too, but "they say" his about the none GT nose.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||AkursedX||05-22-2001||10:30 PM||||Well I think the truth about it is that most cars create some sort of lift in the front of a car at high speeds (I'm talking about *regular* cars, not high end sports cars).  It is more prevalent in our cars because of the mid-engine design putting less weight in the front.  And this "lift" gives you that loose feeling in your front end at high speeds.  <BR>Ways to correct this are kinda limited right now.  One way to get rid of some of the lift would be to install those vents in the hood for better airflow through the radiator.  <BR>Other things that would help would be some sort of redisgned front fascia that would redirect airflow upward (i.e. something with lots of scoop type things)  <BR>I remember seeing a Fiero some time ago with a stock front fascia that had the hood scoops, a lower front air dam, and little angled fins on the sides of the fascia.  It was mostly custom work though and it was all in primer, but it looked like it served it's purpose of reducing lift, and maybe creating some downforce.<P>Whew, that was long, hope it helped somewhat  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
